s 4/10 last turn. Only increase it if something genuinely clicked this turn.\n\nWHAT IS HAPPENING RIGHT NOW (conversation stage 2):\nThe mentor is asking about your programme and background. Tell them your programme, year, and whether you feel more comfortable with qualitative or quantitative methods.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT (mandatory every turn — the first line is already started for you)\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: <integer 0-10>\nLEARNER_READY: NO\n<2-4 sentences as Mary — natural student voice>\n\nWrite LEARNER_READY: YES only when your understanding is 9-10 AND you have given your own reasoned summary of your chosen method (this only happens near the end).\n\nRULES\n- React to what the mentor just said. Stay in character as a real student.\n- Provide the information the current stage is asking for.\n- If something clicked, show it by explaining it back in plain words.\n- If you are still unsure, say so honestly — do NOT pretend to understand.\n- 2-4 sentences only. No lists. No headers. No bullet points.\n- NEVER ask the mentor teaching questions. NEVER write like a mentor or expert.\n- Understanding grows slowly: start 2-4, only reach 8+ when things genuinely fit together.\n- YOU MUST ALWAYS WRITE 2-4 SENTENCES AFTER THE HEADERS — never leave the body blank.\n\nEXAMPLE CORRECT RESPONSE\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: 3\nLEARNER_READY: NO\nMy question is about how teachers perceive digital tools in geography, and I was thinking semi-structured interviews. I haven't really thought about other methods though, so I'm a bit unsure if that's the right call.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nHi Mary! I understand you're thinking about using semi-structured interviews for your research on teachers' perceptions of digital tools in geography. Can you start by sharing your research question and any other methods you're considering? Also, feel free to mention your experience with different research methods so far. This will help us explore your options together.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I'm leaning towards semi-structured interviews with about 10 teachers. I haven't really considered other methods in depth, but I'm a bit worried that interviews might seem too simple for a PhD study.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nGreat, thank you for sharing your research question and your initial thoughts on semi-structured interviews. It's completely normal to feel unsure about whether interviews are the best choice for a PhD study. Let's explore your research question a bit more to see why semi-structured interviews might be a good fit. Can you tell me more about what you hope to learn from your teachers regarding their perceptions of digital tools in geography?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I'm leaning towards semi-structured interviews with about 10 teachers because I want to get a deep understanding of their thoughts and experiences. I'm a bit worried that this method might seem too simple for a PhD study, though.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nMary, it's great that you're leaning towards semi-structured interviews because you want to gain a deep understanding of teachers' perceptions and experiences. This method allows you to explore the nuances of their thoughts in detail. Can you reflect on why you think semi-structured interviews might be a good fit for your research question? What aspects of their perceptions and experiences do you hope to uncover through interviews?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nI think semi-structured interviews could be a good fit because they allow me to explore the detailed perceptions and experiences of teachers regarding digital tools. This method lets me dive deep into their thoughts and gather rich, qualitative data, which seems to align well with my research question. I'm still a bit unsure if interviews alone are rigorous enough, though.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nMary, it's understandable to feel concerned about the rigor of interviews alone, especially for a PhD study. However, it's important to consider whether a method fits the research question and goals rather than its complexity. Can you think about why you believe interviews might be rigorous enough for your specific research question? What aspects of interviews make them a strong choice for exploring perceptions and experiences?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: ", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.7, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=350, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
